==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 04 NOTES — nmap Part 1
Date: 02-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- nmap -sn does HOST DISCOVERY only (who's alive), no port scanning at all
- Default nmap scan checks the top 1000 most common TCP ports
- -F (fast) checks only the top 100 ports - quicker, less thorough
- -p lets you target specific ports, a list, a range, or -p- for ALL 65535
- open = service listening, closed = reachable but nothing listening, filtered = firewall likely blocking
- Only scan machines you own or that explicitly allow it (scanme.nmap.org, your own local network)

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] ip route (finding my subnet)
[ ] sudo nmap -sn <my-subnet>
[ ] nmap localhost
[ ] nmap scanme.nmap.org
[ ] nmap -F scanme.nmap.org
[ ] nmap -p 22 / -p 1-100 scanme.nmap.org

paste your real output here :
